<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>SOC Analyst Lab: Questions, Hints and Final Answers</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Calibri, 'Segoe UI', Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.5; color: #1e293b; margin: 35px; }
    h1 { color: #0f172a; font-size: 22pt; border-bottom: 2px solid #0f172a; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
    .subtitle { color: #64748b; font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 25px; }
    h2 { color: #047857; font-size: 15pt; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding-bottom: 4px; margin-top: 30px; }
    .q-card { border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 20px; background: #ffffff; }
    .q-title { font-weight: bold; color: #0f172a; font-size: 11.5pt; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .hint-box { background: #fffbeb; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; padding: 8px 12px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 10pt; color: #92400e; }
    .ans-box { background: #f0fdf4; border-left: 4px solid #16a34a; padding: 8px 12px; font-size: 10.5pt; color: #166534; }
    code { font-family: Consolas, 'Courier New', monospace; background: #f1f5f9; padding: 2px 5px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 10pt; color: #b91c1c; font-weight: bold; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 15px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
    th { background: #0f172a; color: white; padding: 8px 10px; text-align: left; font-size: 10pt; }
    td { border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 8px 10px; font-size: 10pt; }
    .walkthrough-step { background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; }
    .walkthrough-step strong { color: #0369a1; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>🛡️ SOC Analyst Lab: Web Attack Forensics & IoC Hunting</h1>
  <p class="subtitle"><strong>Official Question Bank, Hints & Final Answers Key</strong></p>

  <p><em>This document contains the complete step-by-step lab walkthrough, followed by the structured Question Bank with Hints and Final Answers formatted for easy copy-pasting into your learning management or CTF platform.</em></p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Part 1: Quick Walkthrough - How to Perform the Lab</h2>
  
  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Step 1: SQL Injection Attack & Detection</strong><br>
    1. In the <strong>Target Application</strong>, go to <strong>"🔍 Catalog & Search"</strong>.<br>
    2. Enter: <code>' UNION SELECT 1,username,password,email,role FROM users--</code><br>
    3. The application leaks the hidden database users table with admin credentials and Flag: <code>BLITZ{5ql1_un10n_d8_3xf1l_9f82c4a17e}</code>.<br>
    4. In the <strong>SOC SIEM Console</strong>, filter by <code>uri:*union*</code> or <code>status:500</code>. Click <strong>"Inspect"</strong> to analyze URL encoding <code>%27</code> and decoded SQL syntax.
  </div>

  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Step 2: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Attack & Detection</strong><br>
    1. In the <strong>Target Application</strong>, go to <strong>"Catalog Search"</strong> (Reflected) or <strong>"Feedback Board"</strong> (Stored).<br>
    2. Submit: <code>&lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</code>.<br>
    3. An execution pop-up triggers in the sandbox displaying Flag: <code>BLITZ{x55_cl13nt_3x3c_7b39d104fa}</code>.<br>
    4. In the <strong>SOC SIEM Console</strong>, filter by <code>category:xss</code> or <code>rule:RULE-SEC-003</code> to observe URL encodings <code>%3C</code> and <code>%3E</code>, and trace the attacker C2 endpoint (<code>http://evil-c2.attacker.com/steal</code>) in log <code>log-017</code>.
  </div>

  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Step 3: Authentication Brute Force Attack & Detection</strong><br>
    1. In the <strong>Target Application</strong>, go to <strong>"🔐 Login Portal"</strong> &rarr; click <strong>"Hydra Burst"</strong>.<br>
    2. Observe 24 rapid failed logins (<code>401 Unauthorized</code>) followed by the valid password <code>dragon123</code> (<code>302 Found</code>).<br>
    3. Logging in unlocks Flag: <code>BLITZ{bruf3_4uth_c0mpr0m153_6e1a4d88}</code>.<br>
    4. In the <strong>SOC SIEM Console</strong>, view the red <code>401</code> volume spike on the histogram and locate the compromise transition from 401 to 302.
  </div>

  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Step 4: Threat Actor Incident Correlation</strong><br>
    1. In the SIEM, filter by <code>ioc:true</code> to correlate events across timestamps.<br>
    2. Identify threat actor IP <code>198.51.100.72</code> accessing unauthorized endpoint <code>/admin/export?table=users_credentials</code>.<br>
    3. Conclude the investigation to obtain master Flag: <code>BLITZ{1nc1d3nt_c0rr3l4t10n_89c2e01b}</code>.
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Part 2: Questions, Hints & Final Answers</h2>

  <!-- Question 1 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 1 (Web Log Fundamentals)</div>
    <p>Which standard 3-digit HTTP response status code indicates an authentication failure (Unauthorized access) when credentials are invalid or missing?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Think of the client error code in the 4xx range returned by web servers when login credentials fail.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>401</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 2 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 2 (Web Log Fundamentals)</div>
    <p>In the standard NCSA/Apache Combined Log Format, which field comes immediately after the HTTP request line (e.g. <code>"GET /catalog/search HTTP/1.1"</code>)?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> It is the 3-digit numerical code indicating whether the request succeeded (200), failed authentication (401), or errored (500).
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>status code</code> <em>(also accept: status, HTTP status, status_code)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 3 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 3 (SQL Injection Forensics)</div>
    <p>What is the two-character hexadecimal URL percent encoding for a single quotation mark (<code>'</code>), commonly seen in access logs when attackers break SQL query strings?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the ASCII table, single quote (') is character 39 in decimal, which equals hex 27. It is prefixed with a percent sign in URLs.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>%27</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 4 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 4 (SQL Injection Forensics)</div>
    <p>Exploit the SQL Injection vulnerability in the Target Application catalog search using a <code>UNION SELECT</code> query. What secret challenge flag is retrieved from the leaked administrator database record?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the Target Application, enter <code>' UNION SELECT 1,username,password,email,role FROM users--</code> into the catalog search bar.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>BLITZ{5ql1_un10n_d8_3xf1l_9f82c4a17e}</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 5 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 5 (SQL Injection Forensics)</div>
    <p>In the SOC SIEM logs, search for automated vulnerability scanning activity. What was the source IP address that executed the automated <code>sqlmap</code> scan against <code>/catalog/search</code>?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the SIEM console search bar, type <code>sqlmap</code> or <code>rule:RULE-SEC-005</code> and look at the Client IP column.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>198.51.100.44</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 6 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 6 (Cross-Site Scripting Forensics)</div>
    <p>What URL percent-encoded string represents the opening angle bracket (<code>&lt;</code>) frequently observed in raw access logs during HTML and script tag injection attacks?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In ASCII, the less-than symbol (&lt;) has decimal value 60, which corresponds to hex 3C.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>%3C</code> <em>(also accept: %3c)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 7 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 7 (Cross-Site Scripting Forensics)</div>
    <p>Trigger the Reflected XSS vulnerability in the Target Application search bar with a script alert payload. What challenge flag is displayed in the client-side execution sandbox modal?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the Target Application catalog search bar, enter <code>&lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</code> and press Search.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>BLITZ{x55_cl13nt_3x3c_7b39d104fa}</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 8 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 8 (Cross-Site Scripting Forensics)</div>
    <p>In the SOC SIEM logs, inspect the stored XSS attack targeting the guestbook endpoint (<code>/board/feedback</code>). What malicious external C2 domain endpoint was the attacker attempting to exfiltrate session cookies to?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Inspect log <code>log-017</code> in the SIEM console and examine the decoded <code>src</code> attribute inside the injected script tag.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>http://evil-c2.attacker.com/steal</code> <em>(also accept: evil-c2.attacker.com/steal, http://evil-c2.attacker.com, evil-c2.attacker.com)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 9 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 9 (Authentication Brute Force Forensics)</div>
    <p>What HTTP response status code anomaly in the SIEM timeline histogram serves as the primary behavioral indicator of an ongoing login brute-force attack?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Look at the 3-digit status code that spikes sharply on the histogram during high-frequency password guessing.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>401</code> <em>(also accept: 401 Unauthorized)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 10 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 10 (Authentication Brute Force Forensics)</div>
    <p>Execute the brute force attack against user <code>admin</code> on the Target Application login portal. What is the cracked legitimate password that yields an HTTP 302 redirect?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Click "Hydra Burst" on the login page or run the Attack Simulator. Check the attempt that authenticates with status 302.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>dragon123</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 11 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 11 (Authentication Brute Force Forensics)</div>
    <p>What challenge flag is unlocked upon successfully authenticating as <code>admin</code> on the Target Application login portal?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Log into the Target Application using username <code>admin</code> and password <code>dragon123</code> (or inject SQLi bypass <code>' OR '1'='1</code>) and read the success message.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>BLITZ{bruf3_4uth_c0mpr0m153_6e1a4d88}</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 12 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 12 (Authentication Brute Force Forensics)</div>
    <p>In the SOC SIEM baseline logs, an attacker conducted an automated brute force attack against the account <code>sarah_admin</code>. What was the attacker's source IP address?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the SIEM search bar, search for <code>sarah_admin</code>. Find the IP that issued 34 consecutive 401 attempts before a single 302 redirect.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>203.0.113.89</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 13 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 13 (Incident Investigation)</div>
    <p>Identify the external threat actor IP address responsible for the multi-stage attack involving XSS injection and administrative data export?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Filter the SIEM by <code>category:xss</code> or inspect logs <code>log-015</code> through <code>log-018</code> to identify the common source IP address.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>198.51.100.72</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 14 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 14 (Incident Investigation)</div>
    <p>What sensitive database table name was targeted in the attacker's unauthorized <code>GET /admin/export</code> request?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Inspect log <code>log-018</code> in the SIEM console and examine the <code>table</code> parameter in the decoded query string.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>users_credentials</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 15 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 15 (Incident Investigation)</div>
    <p>Enter the master Incident Correlation Flag awarded for reconstructing the attacker's full kill chain:</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Master triage flag representing the complete incident correlation.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>BLITZ{1nc1d3nt_c0rr3l4t10n_89c2e01b}</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Part 3: Master Flags Cheat Sheet</h2>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Vulnerability Vector</th>
      <th style="width: 35%;">Trigger / Location</th>
      <th style="width: 40%;">Final Flag</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>SQL Injection</strong></td>
      <td>Catalog Search UNION SELECT</td>
      <td><code>BLITZ{5ql1_un10n_d8_3xf1l_9f82c4a17e}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Cross-Site Scripting</strong></td>
      <td>Catalog Search / Feedback Script Injection</td>
      <td><code>BLITZ{x55_cl13nt_3x3c_7b39d104fa}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Login Compromise</strong></td>
      <td>Login Portal (admin / dragon123)</td>
      <td><code>BLITZ{bruf3_4uth_c0mpr0m153_6e1a4d88}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Incident Correlation</strong></td>
      <td>Full Multi-Stage Attack Triage</td>
      <td><code>BLITZ{1nc1d3nt_c0rr3l4t10n_89c2e01b}</code></td>
    </tr>
  </table>

</body>
</html>
